in ideal world we want to super fast and minimum amount of memory (RAM)!
but we have trade of most of the time.
some times we have more space, so we optimize algorithm and make them fast,
some time we have limited space, like when we have app for a small mobile device!
so we will optimize for the space (scalability is not big factor, we have just 1 user for the app)

Note: the backend and sdk difference is here
      it's time and space!
      Big O can used for time, can used for space!
      in this course we use it for time!
      because it has more challenge.
      but you can use the idea to optimize the memory.

Note: when we talk about space complexity,
      we only look at the "additional" space (input is not the matter!)
      that we should allocate relative to th size of the input





public void log(int[] numbers) {            //the space complexity is O(1)   (just have a single i)
                                            //the runtime complexity is O(n)
        for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
            System.out.println(numbers[i]);
        }
    }

-------------------------------

public void log(int[] numbers) {            //the space complexity is O(n)
                                            //the runtime complexity is O(n)

        int[] copy = new int[numbers.length];           //O(n)

        for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {      //O(1)
            System.out.println(numbers[i]);
        }
    }